#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167794} :activity/id #uuid "fd917f34-57d2-5f05-a29a-d33c8b5c96a5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049957 :topic/id #uuid "21390ce8-05ac-5fcf-ba45-89cb6f33aadc" :topic/math-academy-id 3147} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167790 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166883 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166488 :question/id #uuid "22a44ff4-55e7-51db-a54e-26ef23764fa4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12490"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d13b0d31-65b8-550f-b97b-f4ece63bf834" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281635}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "360dd2dd-b48a-554c-9341-3a3e93d1e334" :step/math-academy-id 44978 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167791}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167791 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168149 :tutorial/content "Recall that the average value of a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $D = [a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_\\textrm{ave} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Length}(D)}\\int_a^b f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThere is an analogous definition for functions of two variables, stated below:\n\n> *Suppose $D$ is a closed and bounded region in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ which has non-zero finite area $\\textrm{Area}(D).$ If $f (x, y)$ is a continuous function on $D,$ then **the average value of $\\boldsymbol f$ on $\\boldsymbol D$** is given by*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> f_{\\textrm{ave}} =\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n> $$\n\nFor example, let's find the average value of the function \n\n\\[\nf(x,y) =x+3y^2+2\n\\]\n\nover the region \n\n\\[\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n\\] \n\nEarlier, we found that \n\n\\[\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = 28, \\qquad \\textrm{Area}(D) = 4.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the average value of the function over [math]D[/math] is\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf_{\\textrm{ave}}  \t\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot 28 \\\\[5pt]\n\n&=  7. \n\\end{align*}" :tutorial/id #uuid "c862ff42-4e5b-5bbb-aed6-3d9760d339b1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7770 :tutorial/title "The Average Value of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "c77b7ad4-9d8d-58f4-bd79-4c0b0b47cfea" :step/math-academy-id 44981 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167792}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167792 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166884 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166489 :question/id #uuid "0a06a8ae-69d6-5ded-979e-149f650a1676" :question/math-academy-id "e-12491"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b43eea5b-9da3-5fe0-a28c-cc01aed3627a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374234}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "c0151bcd-ba24-5681-9ec6-52888fa06a36" :step/math-academy-id 44982 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167793}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167793 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166885 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166490 :question/id #uuid "13800660-08b4-50af-93b4-1013bbf41e66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12492"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e5deee-e910-5bb1-9d9a-0b24886ab6c1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374241}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Polar Region"} :step/id #uuid "a1446b8a-1f48-5f9e-b01f-42db997299c2" :step/math-academy-id 44985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167794 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168150 :tutorial/content "Given a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $[a, b],$ the mean value theorem for integrals guarantees that there exists a number $c \\in [a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x = f(c) \\cdot (b-a),\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0afb9b15787eb89f73f322290bdbe2288027c54924d274c438773ce151a17291.png)\n\nWe have a similar theorem for functions with two variables. The so-called **mean value theorem for double integrals** states:\n\n> *If the function $f(x,y)$ is continuous on a closed and bounded region $D,$ then there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n> $$\n\nThis has a nice geometrical interpretation when $f$ is non-negative on $D.$ In this case, the theorem tells us that there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ \n\nThe diagram below shows the case when $D$ is rectangular, and the corresponding cylinder is a rectangular solid.\n\n![](images/8e/8ed477207ee61fbdaf963ed8a127e3f1d4cf76707d2eeb4b450fd49f78e15b7e.png)\n\nIn general, the point $(a,b)$ mentioned in the theorem is not unique. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "b65eeb5f-8a44-55b9-819c-1c6c0d979d1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7778 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "037c5a22-eee3-557c-b683-370d2389ad82" :step/math-academy-id 45031 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167795}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167795 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168151 :tutorial/content "Consider the continuous function \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x+3y^2+2\n$$\n\nand the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(x+3{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+3{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[xy+{y}^{3}+2y]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}+12x]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, our mean-value equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =f(a,b)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 7 & =f(a,b) \\\\ 7 & =a+3{b}^{2}+2 \\\\ a & =5-3{b}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation describes part of a parabola in the $xy$-plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46873c4ecb290380488e589082fc0759324d3540494f5b83e2d64db5d9cdf62a.png)\n\nTo find a particular point that satisfies the mean value theorem, we can set, for example, $b=1 \\in [0,2]$ and obtain $a=2.$ So, the point $(2,1)$ satisfies the mean value theorem for double integrals. Consequently, the two solids below must have the same volume.\n\n![](images/82/82d3a762ab323339570e6010038e75ce95f3fdbde958a2ffa876972cbf91e8fb.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "b890c915-3364-5ac3-b88b-298ed92d9def" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7784 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "32b5b570-9467-5bff-bc3b-29964ed86c09" :step/math-academy-id 45050 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167790}}] :activity/title "The Average Value of a Multivariable Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166974}]}
